      "section": "methods",
      "text": "Moreover, they utilize waste biomass like crop residues and sewage sludge, promoting waste-to-resource management [ 18 ]. While extensive research exists on the individual adsorption of PFAS and toxic metals, the simultaneous co-adsorption dynamics in multi-contaminant systems remain critically under - studied. This is a significant gap, as real-world contaminated water matrices invariably contain multiple pollutants. The presence of other pollutants, such as toxic metals, could influence PFAS adsorption via competitive or synergistic interactions [ 20 ]. Given the potential coex - istence of these contaminants, this study aims to address this gap by studying the simultaneous removal of PFAS and toxic metals using carbonaceous materials. For this purpose, the present study focuses on the simultaneous removal of three PFAS- including PFOA, PFOS and GenX (as an alter - native of PFOA) - and a commonly found heavy metal, cadmium (Cd (II)). Cadmium is chosen due to its widespread occurrence and docu - mented detrimental effects on aquatic ecosystems, soil-plant systems, and human health [ 21 ]. This study compared the efficiency of activated carbon with three distinct biochars (derived from wood and sewage sludge pyrolyzed at different temperatures) under batch conditions. A comprehensive evaluation of sorption isotherms, interplay of PFAS and"
