      "text": "for 2 h, and utilized for subsequent cycles. Three adsorption- regeneration cycles were conducted, followed by sample analyses. 2.4. Adsorption efficiency in real water matrices To further assess the effectiveness of these adsorbents, PFAS removal was also assessed in different water matrices including 1 local ground - water (collected in Brittany, France) and two laundry wastewater sam - ples (provided by a laundry in Gard, France) spiked with PFAS. Details on the sampling site and characterization procedures are provided in our previous study [ 29 ]. To simulate the conditions of concentrated PFAS waste streams requiring treatment, these water samples were spiked with PFOA, PFOS, and GenX at concentrations of 1, 5, 10, and 20 mg L \u0000 1 of each. Adsorption experiments were conducted using 1 g L \u0000 1 of each adsorbent over a 24-h contact period. The characteristics of the tested water samples are provided in Table 2 . The pH of the groundwater ex - periments remained stable throughout the batch tests due to its inherent buffering capacity. 2.5. Analytical characterization A liquid chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry system (LC- MS/MS, Waters) was used to analyze the target PFAS (PFOA, PFOS, and GenX) as detailed previously [ 19 ]. Instrumental and background"
